import Foundation
import FTEngine

// Chapters 9–10: Advanced Reasoning and Final Table Mastery.

func levelsChapter9() -> [Scenario] { [
    lvl("L081", ch: 9, t: "The Story So Far", d: 8,
        obj: "Read the whole timeline. Does their line make sense any other way?",
        learn: "Action sequences narrate a range.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "Qs Qh", board: "Ad 8c 4s 2h 2d",
        seats: [St(.final, 5200), St(.middle, 5600, .balanced)],
        pool: 2200, req: 900, hist: [.ch(1, 900)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.withdraw: .excellent, .match: .weak, .challenge: .majorError],
        rec: .withdraw,
        vars: [V.profile, V.category],
        quick: "A balanced player has now bet every street of an ace-high board, growing the size each time. Queens under an ace have heard the whole story: fold.",
        coach: "Single actions lie; sequences struggle to. A balanced profile betting three growing barrels through A-8-4-2-2 tells one coherent story — a strong ace or better, strengthened by the paired deuce. The bluffs that start this line give up by the fourth card. Your queens beat exactly the abandoned bluffs (which stopped betting) and lose to everything still talking. Read lines like sentences, not words.",
        mistake: "Re-evaluating each street in isolation and calling three times 'because queens are good'.",
        take: "A range is a story told one street at a time. By the river, believe it.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Have them pass the middle street then suddenly fire big: the broken story adds bluffs and weak aces back in, and matching becomes defensible.", varyR: .match),

    lvl("L082", ch: 9, t: "Ranges, Not Hands", d: 8,
        obj: "Stop guessing their hand. Score the whole range against yours.",
        learn: "Category-weighted range reasoning.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "Jh Js", board: "9d 5c 2s",
        seats: [St(.final, 5400), St(.early, 5100, .aggressive)],
        pool: 1100, req: 600, hist: [.ch(1, 600)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.match: .excellent, .challenge: .strong, .withdraw: .weak],
        rec: .match,
        probQ: "Chance your jacks are best against this profile's whole challenging range",
        probPct: 58.0, probBasis: "behavioralAssumption",
        assume: ["Weights the aggressive range: overpairs and sets (you lose), top-pair nines and draws (you win), pure pressure (you win big)."],
        vars: [V.profile, V.category],
        quick: "Don't put them on A-9 or on a bluff — put them on everything at once. Against the weighted whole, jacks are a 58% favorite. Match.",
        coach: "The amateur question is 'what do they have?' The expert question is 'what is the ratio?' An aggressive range on this dry board is roughly: a quarter hands that beat jacks, a third nine-x you dominate, the rest air pressure. You don't need to know which — the weighted average says continue. Matching (rather than raising) keeps every bluff in their range bluffing, which is where most of your 58% lives.",
        mistake: "Picking a single hand to fear or a single bluff to hope for, then betting the round on your guess.",
        take: "You never play against a hand. You always play against a ratio.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Give them a cautious profile: the ratio inverts (half sets and overpairs), and jacks quietly fold.", varyR: .withdraw),

    lvl("L083", ch: 9, t: "The Card You Hold", d: 8,
        obj: "Your ace of spades isn't just a card — it's a subtraction from their range.",
        learn: "Card removal: your holdings edit their possibilities.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "As Kd", board: "Ks Js 7s 2c",
        seats: [St(.final, 5600), St(.middle, 5300, .balanced)],
        pool: 1400, req: 800, hist: [.ch(1, 800)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.match: .excellent, .withdraw: .defensible, .challenge: .weak],
        rec: .match,
        probQ: "Chance the opponent holds two spades (a made flush)",
        probN: 36, probD: 1035, probPct: 3.5,
        assume: ["Exact over uniform random hands; real betting ranges are denser in spades — treat as a floor, not a forecast."],
        vars: [V.removal, V.category],
        quick: "Top pair, top kicker on a three-spade board sounds grim — but YOU hold the ace of spades. The nut flush doesn't exist, and made flushes are down to 3.5% of random hands.",
        coach: "Card removal is subtraction with consequences: holding the A♠ deletes every nut-flush combination from their range and shrinks all flushes from 45 combos to 36. It also means their scariest bets are more often king-x you outkick, or spade-draw semi-bluffs you're ahead of. Bluff-catching with top pair PLUS the key blocker is a different hand than top pair alone — the same 800 that folds K-Q calls comfortably with A♠-K.",
        mistake: "Reading the board's texture without subtracting the cards in your own hand from it.",
        take: "Every card you hold is a card they can't. Do the subtraction.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Swap your A♠ for the A♦: the nut flush returns to their range, the blocker math dies, and folding becomes recommended.", varyR: .withdraw),

    lvl("L084", ch: 9, t: "Removal as a Weapon", d: 9,
        obj: "You hold no flush and (almost) no hand. You also hold the most important card in the deck. Use it.",
        learn: "Blockers arm polarized challenges.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "As 5d", board: "Ks 9s 4s 2h",
        seats: [St(.final, 6800), St(.early, 5900, .balanced)],
        pool: 1700, req: 700, hist: [.ch(1, 700)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.challenge: .excellent, .withdraw: .strong, .match: .weak],
        rec: .challenge, recSize: 1.35, band: 1.2...1.5, stops: [1.0, 1.2, 1.35, 1.5],
        probQ: "Chance the opponent holds two spades (a made flush)",
        probN: 36, probD: 1035, probPct: 3.5,
        assume: ["Uniform-random floor. Without your A♠ it would be 4.3% — and would include the nut flush."],
        vars: [V.removal, V.profile, V.poolOdds],
        quick: "Your A♠ makes the nut flush impossible for them and available for you to represent. A big re-challenge tells the one story they must respect.",
        coach: "The polarized re-raise: ace-high can't win a showdown it calls (matching is the one bad option), but holding the A♠ means their made flushes are rarer AND capped below the nuts — while your line credibly claims exactly the hand they can't have. A balanced profile facing 1.2–1.5× pool with a capped range folds kings, folds two pair, folds small flushes some of the time. Folding your own hand is fine (Strong); weaponizing the blocker is the excellent line.",
        mistake: "Seeing A-high, thinking 'I have nothing,' and withdrawing — evaluating your showdown hand when the decision is about your challenge hand.",
        take: "Sometimes the card that matters is the one nobody else can have.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Swap your A♠ for the A♦: now they CAN hold the nut flush, the story collapses, and withdrawing becomes recommended.", varyR: .withdraw),

    lvl("L085", ch: 9, t: "Polar or Merged?", d: 9,
        obj: "An overbet arrives. First question: what SHAPE is their range?",
        learn: "Classify bets as polarized or merged before responding.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "Kc Qh", board: "Qs 9h 3d 8c",
        seats: [St(.final, 5800), St(.late, 6200, .balanced)],
        pool: 900, req: 1500, hist: [.ch(1, 1500)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.match: .excellent, .withdraw: .defensible, .challenge: .majorError],
        rec: .match,
        vars: [V.profile, V.poolOdds],
        quick: "A 1.7× overbet is a polarized shape: monsters and bluffs, nothing between. Top pair, good kicker is a pure bluff-catcher — and the shape says catch.",
        coach: "Bet sizes have shapes: merged bets (half-pool-ish) come from many medium hands; overbets come from the poles — hands that fear nothing or hands worth nothing. Against a polar range, your K-Q's value is exactly its bluff-catching: it beats every bluff and loses to every monster, so the price decides. 1,500 to win 3,900 needs 38.5% — and balanced polar ranges near the river must bluff close to that often to work. Raising is the one catastrophe: it folds their bluffs and pays their monsters.",
        mistake: "Folding all bluff-catchers to big bets ('it's so big, they must have it') — the size that SHOULD scare you is the merged medium one.",
        take: "First classify the shape. Polar ranges are caught, not out-muscled.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Shrink their bet to a third of the pool: that's a merged shape full of medium hands beating K-Q, and folding becomes right.", varyR: .withdraw),

    lvl("L086", ch: 9, t: "The Delayed Challenge", d: 9,
        obj: "The strongest play this street might be silence. Plan two streets ahead.",
        learn: "Delayed aggression as a line, not a mood.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "Kh Ks", board: "Kd 6c 2s",
        seats: [St(.early, 6100), St(.final, 5700, .aggressive)],
        pool: 1000, req: 0, hist: [],
        opts: [.pass, .challenge, .withdraw],
        tiers: [.pass: .excellent, .challenge: .defensible, .withdraw: .majorError],
        rec: .pass,
        vars: [V.position, V.profile],
        quick: "Top set on a bone-dry board, an aggressive player behind: pass. Your hand fears nothing, and their aggression needs room to hang itself.",
        coach: "The delayed challenge is a designed line: on K-6-2 rainbow your set can't be outdrawn by anything that arrives cheaply, so betting only folds out the air an aggressive profile would otherwise bluff with. Pass now, let them attack the 'weakness', then raise the NEXT street when the pool has grown and their story is committed. Betting immediately is defensible (pools want building) — but against this profile, patience is the bigger pool.",
        mistake: "Confusing slow-play (a mood) with a delayed line (a plan with a trigger and an exit).",
        take: "Aggression is a resource — sometimes you spend theirs instead of yours.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Wet the board to K-6-2 with two hearts: free cards become expensive, and the immediate challenge takes over as excellent.", varyR: .challenge),

    lvl("L087", ch: 9, t: "The Impossible Hand", d: 9,
        obj: "They're representing a monster. Count how many of those actually exist.",
        learn: "Combinatorics can make a claimed hand nearly impossible.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "Ad Ac", board: "As Ah 7d 4c 2s",
        seats: [St(.final, 5900), St(.middle, 6300, .aggressive)],
        pool: 2400, req: 2000, hist: [.ch(1, 2000)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match],
        tiers: [.match: .excellent, .withdraw: .majorError],
        rec: .match,
        vars: [V.removal, V.category],
        quick: "You hold quad aces. Their shove represents... what, exactly? You hold every ace. Count their combos: zero beat you. Match and say thank you.",
        coach: "The extreme case teaches the method: before folding to ANY story, count the combinations that tell it truthfully. Here the count is literally zero — you hold all four aces, the board can't make a straight flush, and nothing beats quads. Every real decision is a softer version of this count: 'they rep the flush — how many flush combos survive my blockers and their line?' When the count approaches zero, the bet approaches a gift.",
        mistake: "Feeling fear at a big bet without counting whether the feared hand can exist.",
        take: "Fear is not a number. Count the combos.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Give yourself kings instead: now their aces-full combos exist and the same shove becomes a genuine — and losing — decision.", varyR: .withdraw),

    lvl("L088", ch: 9, t: "Mixed by Design", d: 9,
        obj: "Two answers are correct on purpose. Understand why that's the lesson.",
        learn: "Mixed strategies: some spots author two excellent lines.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "8h 7h", board: "As 6h 5d",
        seats: [St(.final, 5500), St(.early, 5800, .balanced)],
        pool: 800, req: 400, hist: [.ch(1, 400)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.match: .excellent, .challenge: .excellent, .withdraw: .weak],
        rec: .match,
        probQ: "Chance you complete a straight by the last card",
        probN: 340, probD: 1081, probPct: 31.5,
        assume: ["Any nine or four completes you; backdoor hearts add uncounted equity."],
        vars: [V.poolOdds, V.profile],
        quick: "Match and challenge are BOTH excellent here — by design. A strong draw in position profits quietly or loudly; what matters is knowing both doors are open.",
        coach: "Some spots have one answer; this isn't one. Your open-ended draw with backdoor hearts can match (great price, position, disguise) or semi-bluff raise (fold-out power now, full equity when matched) — and a strong player MIXES deliberately so neither line marks their hand. The scored lesson: if you always raise draws, your raises stop scaring anyone; if you never do, your raises only ever mean monsters. The withdrawal is the only mistake in the room.",
        mistake: "Believing every puzzle — every spot — has exactly one right answer to memorize.",
        take: "Unpredictability isn't chaos; it's two correct answers, alternated on purpose.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Make the price 900 into 800: the match side of the mix collapses, and the semi-bluff stands alone.", varyR: .challenge),

    lvl("L089", ch: 9, t: "Reverse Read", d: 10,
        obj: "You've been the aggressor all hand. What does YOUR line look like from their seat?",
        learn: "Model your own perceived range.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "6c 6s", board: "Ks Qd 4h 2c",
        seats: [St(.final, 6200), St(.middle, 5900, .balanced)],
        pool: 1600, req: 0, hist: [.p(1)],
        opts: [.pass, .challenge, .withdraw],
        tiers: [.challenge: .excellent, .pass: .defensible, .withdraw: .weak],
        rec: .challenge, recSize: 0.65, band: 0.55...0.75, stops: [0.4, 0.55, 0.65, 0.75],
        vars: [V.position, V.profile],
        quick: "Your sixes missed — but your STORY didn't. You raised before the deal and bet the K-Q flop; to them, this new card changed nothing. Keep telling it.",
        coach: "The reverse read: they aren't playing your cards, they're playing your line — and your line (pre-deal raise, flop challenge on K-Q, position) looks exactly like the kings and queens that keep betting here. Their pass caps their range at medium pairs and gives you the pen. A second barrel at two-thirds folds out the hands beating sixes (fours-x never continues; middle pairs squirm out). Passing turns your story into a shrug and invites a river bluff AT you.",
        mistake: "Playing your actual hand mid-story — the table can only see the narrative, and yours is winning.",
        take: "Your range is a character you're playing. Don't break it on the wrong street.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Have them CHALLENGE into you instead of passing: your story meets a better one, and sixes exit gracefully.", varyR: .withdraw),

    lvl("L090", ch: 9, t: "Reasoning Exam", d: 10,
        obj: "Certificate level: blockers, ranges, and a sequence — one decision holds all three.",
        learn: "Certificate level: integrated advanced reasoning.", skill: "Advanced reasoning",
        hole: "Ah Kh", board: "Qh Jh 4s 8d",
        seats: [St(.final, 6500), St(.early, 6100, .balanced)],
        pool: 2000, req: 800, hist: [.ch(1, 800)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.challenge: .excellent, .match: .defensible, .withdraw: .weak],
        rec: .challenge, recSize: 1.2, band: 1.0...1.4, stops: [0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4],
        probQ: "Chance you complete a flush or straight on the final card",
        probN: 12, probD: 46, probPct: 26.1,
        assume: ["Nine hearts + three non-heart tens; the flush would be the best possible; an ace or king pairing may also win, uncounted."],
        vars: [V.removal, V.poolOdds, V.category],
        quick: "Twelve outs to the nuts, two blockers to their monsters, and a capped balanced range: raise. Every advanced tool points the same direction.",
        coach: "The exam stacks the chapter: RANGE — a balanced second barrel on this board is merged, full of Q-x and J-x that fold to real pressure. REMOVAL — your A♥K♥ blocks the nut flush draw and both top two-pair combos they'd continue with. SEQUENCE — their sizing down on the fourth card whispers medium strength. And your own 26% to the absolute nuts backstops the whole play: this is the semi-bluff where you win the pool now, or win it bigger later. Matching is the passive runner-up; folding wastes the best-constructed raise in the book.",
        mistake: "Using each tool separately and stopping at 'draw + price = call' when the range and blocker math scream for the raise.",
        take: "Mastery is when removal, ranges, and the story all cast the same vote — and you count all three.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Make their fourth-street bet a 1.5× overbet instead: the polar shape flips the vote, and flat matching your draw becomes the line.", varyR: .match)
] }

func levelsChapter10() -> [Scenario] { [
    lvl("L091", ch: 10, t: "Six Remain", d: 9,
        obj: "Multiway, pressure, and profiles in one pot. Weigh them in order.",
        learn: "Integrate field size, profiles, and standing.", skill: "Mastery",
        hole: "Ad Jd", board: "",
        seats: [St(.middle, 4400), St(.early, 7100, .aggressive), St(.late, 4000, .cautious),
                St(.final, 3800, .balanced), St(.late, 1500, .balanced), St(.early, 5200, .unpredictable)],
        pool: 1700, req: 700, hist: [.ch(1, 700), .m(2)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.withdraw: .excellent, .match: .weak, .challenge: .defensible],
        rec: .withdraw,
        vars: [V.standing, V.multiway, V.profile],
        quick: "The aggressive open means little — but the cautious MATCH behind it, three players still live, and a short stack burning at the table all say the same thing: not this hand.",
        coach: "Order the evidence like a master: (1) the cautious call is the table's most honest signal, and it caps your A-J's prospects; (2) three seats still act behind you — multiway squeeze risk on a dominated-when-called hand; (3) the 1,500 stack pays forced commitments soon, so your standing appreciates by folding. Any one factor is survivable; all three together make this the cleanest fold in the chapter. Re-squeezing (defensible) at least uses the aggression the spot invites — matching uses nothing.",
        mistake: "Finding one reason to play (a pretty suited ace) and stopping the count before the three reasons not to.",
        take: "At the final table, decisions are votes — count every voter.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Remove the cautious matcher and the short stack: heads-up against aggression with no jump pending, A-J becomes a re-challenge.", varyR: .challenge),

    lvl("L092", ch: 10, t: "The Chip Ladder", d: 9,
        obj: "The pool offers a great price. The ladder offers a better one. Choose currencies.",
        learn: "Pool odds versus standing value, head-on.", skill: "Mastery",
        hole: "Kd Qd", board: "",
        seats: [St(.late, 3000), St(.final, 8200, .aggressive), St(.early, 1100, .cautious), St(.middle, 2900, .balanced)],
        pool: 1500, req: 2800, hist: [.ch(1, 2800)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match],
        tiers: [.withdraw: .excellent, .match: .defensible],
        rec: .withdraw,
        probQ: "Chance K-Q suited wins against this covering pressure range",
        probPct: 48.0, probBasis: "behavioralAssumption",
        assume: ["The aggressive leader's range is wide, but wide ranges still flip with K-Q at best."],
        vars: [V.standing, V.poolOdds],
        quick: "In game points this call is nearly free money. In ranking points it's lighting the ladder on fire: a 1,100 stack is about to hand you a paid rung for nothing.",
        coach: "This is the purest currency exchange in the game: the pool lays you a price (2,800 to win 4,300) that K-Q's ~48% ALMOST justifies in points — and standing value vetoes it flatly. You're covered, the caller's range beats a flip, and third place is nearly locked by the cautious short stack's clock. The Standing Value Model prices the fold at roughly +9 ranking points over the call. When the two currencies disagree, the ranked game's answer wins — every time the rewards are ranked.",
        mistake: "Computing immaculate pool odds in the wrong currency.",
        take: "A good price in points can be a terrible price in standing. Know which game you're in.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Flatten the rewards (pure points, no rankings): the same numbers make matching correct — the veto only exists in ranked play.", varyR: .match),

    lvl("L093", ch: 10, t: "Reading Three Streets Deep", d: 9,
        obj: "A full sequence, a final barrel. Assemble the whole story before you answer it.",
        learn: "Full-line range walking under pressure.", skill: "Mastery",
        hole: "Th Ts", board: "8c 4d 2s 7h 3c",
        seats: [St(.final, 5100), St(.middle, 5700, .balanced)],
        pool: 2600, req: 900, hist: [.ch(1, 900)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.match: .excellent, .withdraw: .defensible, .challenge: .weak],
        rec: .match,
        probQ: "Chance your overpair is best against the full three-street range",
        probPct: 57.0, probBasis: "behavioralAssumption",
        assume: ["Walks the balanced range street by street: missed overcards and busted straight draws now outnumber the slow-played monsters."],
        vars: [V.profile, V.category, V.poolOdds],
        quick: "Walk their line: bet the dry flop (wide), bet the blank turn (narrower), bet the 3 river (bluffs + value). On THIS runout, the busted draws outnumber the monsters. Call.",
        coach: "Three-street reading is subtraction street by street: the 8-4-2 opener includes every overcard hand; the 7 turn kept straight draws drawing; the 3 river completed nothing. What's left that bets three times? Sets and overpairs (you lose), 9-8/A-8 (you beat), and — decisively — every A-K, A-Q, and busted 6-5 that must bluff to win. Your tens block nothing important, but the runout itself blocked THEIR value. 900 into 3,500 needs 26%; the walked range gives you twice that.",
        mistake: "Reading only the river bet ('big = strong') after the runout quietly deleted their value hands.",
        take: "By the river, the board has edited their story. Read the final draft, not the cover.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Make the river the 6 (completing 6-5 and A-5): now the draws ARRIVED instead of busting, and tens fold to the same bet.", varyR: .withdraw),

    lvl("L094", ch: 10, t: "The Short Stack's Last Stand", d: 9,
        obj: "Three commitments left. Every fold is a bet on nothing. Pick your moment — it's now.",
        learn: "Terminal short-stack commitment.", skill: "Mastery",
        hole: "Kc Jc", board: "",
        seats: [St(.late, 900), St(.middle, 6400, .balanced), St(.final, 5800, .cautious), St(.early, 4900, .balanced, out: true)],
        pool: 300, req: 200, hist: [.w(3)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.challenge: .excellent, .withdraw: .weak, .match: .majorError],
        rec: .challenge, recSize: 3.0, band: 2.8...3.0, stops: [3.0],
        vars: [V.standing, V.stackDepth],
        quick: "K-J suited at three commitments is a gift — shove it. The pool alone rebuilds a third of your stack, and both live cards play against anything that calls.",
        coach: "At three commitments the ladder math flips: you're past the depth where folding preserves anything (the next two forced posts consume you), so standing value now MAXIMIZES by gambling while your shove still buys folds. K-J suited crushes the urgency threshold — two live broadways, suited, a blocker to K-K. The trap is 'matching to see cards cheap': it spends a third of your stack without the fold power that is your entire remaining edge.",
        mistake: "Waiting for aces at a depth where three more folds IS elimination.",
        take: "The last stand isn't reckless — it's the only round where all-in is free.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Give yourself twelve commitments: urgency relaxes, and K-J returns to a normal open — commitment is depth-dated.", varyR: .challenge),

    lvl("L095", ch: 10, t: "Four Left, You're Third", d: 10,
        obj: "Attack the second stack? Or protect third place? The sizing knows.",
        learn: "Mid-ladder risk selection.", skill: "Mastery",
        hole: "Ac Tc", board: "",
        seats: [St(.middle, 2800), St(.late, 4600, .balanced), St(.early, 1000, .cautious), St(.final, 6800, .aggressive)],
        pool: 900, req: 900, hist: [.ch(1, 900)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match, .challenge],
        tiers: [.withdraw: .excellent, .challenge: .weak, .match: .weak],
        rec: .withdraw,
        vars: [V.standing, V.stackDepth],
        quick: "A-10 against the SECOND stack — the one player whose call can hurt you with a range that beats you. The short stack pays your patience; decline.",
        coach: "Mid-ladder selection: your opponents are not interchangeable. Fighting the 1,000 stack risks little and gains a rung; fighting the leader is at least honest about the stakes; but the second stack is the poison matchup — they cover you, their bet into a pressure table means it, and beating them changes your rank less than losing to them ends it. A-10 offsuit against a real range from the one player you must not double up: this is how third places become fourths.",
        mistake: "Choosing fights by your cards' beauty instead of by which stack is offering the fight.",
        take: "At four left, WHO you gamble with matters more than what you gamble with.",
        varyP: "What changes the answer?", varyE: "Make the same challenge come from the 1,000-point short stack: calling wide becomes correct — their range is desperate and their loss is your rung.", varyR: .match),

    lvl("L096", ch: 10, t: "The Leader's Dilemma", d: 10,
        obj: "You're the big stack. The mid-stack rock just shoved into you. Punish or preserve?",
        learn: "Leaders exploit widely but respect honest commitment.", skill: "Mastery",
        hole: "Ah Jh", board: "",
        seats: [St(.final, 8400), St(.middle, 2400, .cautious), St(.late, 3100, .balanced), St(.early, 1200, .balanced)],
        pool: 300, req: 2400, hist: [.ch(1, 2400)],
        opts: [.withdraw, .match],
        tiers: [.withdraw: .excellent, .match: .weak],
        rec: .withdraw,
        vars: [V.standing, V.profile],
        quick: "Your job as leader is pressuring FOLDS — not paying off the one range that never folds. A cautious mid-stack's all-in is the top of the deck. Let it go.",
        coach: "The leader's edge is asymmetric pressure: raise wide, fold to strength, repeat. It fails the moment you start 'defending' against honest commitment — a cautious profile jamming ten commitments into the chip leader holds premium pairs and A-K, against which A-J is drawing thin. Losing costs little of your stack but much of your leverage (a doubled resistor emboldens the table). Keep the crown cheap: bully the folds, respect the stands, and let the 1,200 stack's clock keep working for you.",
